'''tother''' (tuᴛ̵ʜ′ėr), ''indef. pron.'' [A form arising from a misdivision of ''that other'', ME. also ''thet other'', as ''the tother''. So ''tone'', in ''the tone'', for ''that one'', ''thet one'' (see ''tone''<sup>2</sup>). ''Tother'' is often written ''t'other'', as if it were a contraction of ''the other''.] Other: originally and usually preceded by ''the'', with ''the tone'' in the preceding clause. See the etymology, and compare ''tone''<sup>2</sup>.

{{smaller block|And ''the tother'' Hond he lifteth up azenst the Est, in tokene to manace the Mysdoeres.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 9.}}
Ffor right dedely ''the tone'' hatid ''the toder''.
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2337.}}
How happy could I be with either,
Were ''t'other'' dear charmer away.
{{right|''Gay'', Beggar's Opera, ii. 2.}}}}

'''totidem verbis''' (tot′i-dem vėr′bis). [L., in so many words: ''totidem'', just so many (< ''tot'', so many, + demonstrative suffix ''-dem''); ''verbis'', abl. pl. of ''verbum'', word: see ''verb''.] In so many words; in the very words.

